{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_b23e86c6-37fb-44e8-9005-6d258a53efb5","role":"user","created":1775329562,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_eba6f5bad5868425","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"65:| FO-2. - Computed interest charge and computed interest rate on interest-bearing public debt | 21 | LIABILITIES TO FOREIGNERS REPORTED BY NONBANKING BUSINESS ENTERPRISES IN THE UNITED STATES | LIABILITIES TO FOREIGNERS REPORTED BY NONBANKING BUSINESS ENTERPRISES IN THE UNITED STATES |\n66:| FD-3. - Interest-bearing public debt | 22 | CM-IV-1. - Total liabilities by type | 86 |\n68:| FD-5. - Interest-bearing securities issued by Government agencies | 24 | CM-IV-3. - Total liabilities by type of country | 88 |\n70:| FO-7. - Maturity distribution and average length of marketable interest-bearing public debt | 26 | CM-V-1. - Total claims by type | 89 |\n75:| PDO-1. - Maturity schedule of interest-bearing marketable public debt securities | 30 | CM-VI-2. - Foreign purchases and sales of long-term foreign securities | 92 |\n96:| TSO-2. - Interest-bearing marketable public debt securities by type and maturity distribution | 56 | FCP-III-3. - Consolidated monthly bank positions | 106 |\n97:| TSO-3. - Interest-bearing marketable public debt securities by issue | 56 | GERMAN MARK POSITIONS | GERMAN MARK POSITIONS |\n240:On February 10 the Treasury announced that it would auction $5,250 million of 2-year notes to refund $3,907 million of notes maturing February 28, 1982, and to raise $1,343 million new cash. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series P-1984, to be dated March 1, 1982, due February 29, 1984, with interest payable on August 31, 1982; February 28 and August 31, 1983; and February 29, 1984. A coupon rate of 15-1/8% was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\n250:On February 16 the Treasury announced that it would auction $3,250 million of 5-year 2-month notes to raise new cash. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series E-1987, to be dated March 3, 1982, due May 15, 1987, with interest payable on November 15 and May 15 in each year until maturity. A coupon rate of 14% was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\n278:Treasury bills are sold on a discount basis under competitive and noncompetitive bidding, and at maturity their par amount is payable without interest. All series are issued entirely in book-entry form in a minimum amount of $10,000 and in any higher $5,000 multiple, on the records either of the Federal Reserve banks and branches, or of the Department of the Treasury.\n617:| Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Justice Department > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Labor Department > Unnamed: 2_level_1 | State Department > Unnamed: 3_level_1 | Transportation Department > Unnamed: 4_level_1 | Treasury Department > Interest on the public debt | Treasury Department > Other | Environmental Protection Agency > Unnamed: 7_level_1 |\n690:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Health and Human Services > Federal old-age, disability, and hospital insurance | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Office of Personnel Management > Civil service retirement and disability fund | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Other > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Federal employer contributions to retirement funds > Total > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Defense Department > Soldiers' and Airmen's Home permanent fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal old-age and survivors trust fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal disability insurance trust fund | Interest credited to certain Government accounts > Health and Human Services > Federal hospital insurance trust fund | Unnamed: 9_level_0 > Health and Human Services > Federal supplementary medical insurance trust fund 2/ |\n755:3/ Includes adjustment of interest on public debt issues to convert to the accrual basis. Also, includes Indian tribal funds, judicial survivors annuity fund, foreign service retirement and disability fund, airport and airway trust fund, and other.\n864:| Interest | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan | nan |\n983:| Classification (trust funds) > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Outlays of trust funds > (9) | Interfund transactions > Total 4/ (10) | Interfund transactions > Federal payments and contributions (11) | Interfund transactions > Federal employer contributions (12) | Interfund transactions > Interest and profits on investments in Federal securities (13) | Interfund transactions > Other (14) | Interfund transactions > (15) | Unified budget outlays attributable to trust funds > (16) | Excess of receipts, or outlays (-) > Unnamed: 9_level_1 |\n1278:Under authority of Public Law 95-147, the Treasury implemented a program on November 2, 1978, to invest a portion of its operating cash in obligations of depositaries maintaining tax and loan accounts. Under the Treasury tax and loan investment program, depositary financial institutions select the manner in which they will participate in the program. Depositaries that wish to retain funds deposited in their tax and loan accounts in interest-bearing obligations participate under the Note Option; depositaries that wish to remit the funds to the Treasury's account at Federal Reserve banks participate under the Remittance Option.\n1288:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Federal Reserve banks | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Treasury tax and loan note accounts | Treasury operating balance 1/ Available funds in demand accounts at: > Other depositaries 2/ | Total 3/ > Unnamed: 4_level_1 | Funds in process of collection through- > Federal Reserve banks | Funds in process of collection through- > Other depositaries 4/ | Time deposits and other interest-bearing demand accounts > Unnamed: 7_level_1 | Other demand accounts 5/ > Unnamed: 8_level_1 | Coin, currency, and coinage metal > Unnamed: 9_level_1 | Miscellaneous items > Unnamed: 10_level_1 | Gold balance 6/ > Unnamed: 11_level_1 | Balance in account of the U.S. Treasury > Unnamed: 12_level_1 |\n1541:Table FD-2. - Computed Interest Charge and Computed Interest Rate on Interest-Bearing Public Debt (Dollar amounts in millions)\n1543:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Total interest-bearing public debt 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_1 > Unnamed: 1_level_2 | Computed annual interest charge 2/ > Unnamed: 2_level_1 > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | Total interest-bearing public debt 1/ > Unnamed: 3_level_1 > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Computed annual interest rate > Total > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Computed annual interest rate > Marketable issues > Treasury bills 3/ | Computed annual interest rate > Unnamed: 6_level_1 > Treasury notes | Computed annual interest rate > Unnamed: 7_level_1 > Treasury bonds | Nonmarketable > Other 4/ > Unnamed: 8_level_2 | Nonmarketable > Government account series > Unnamed: 9_level_2 |\n1569:Source: Bureau of Government Financial Operations. Note: The computed annual interest charge represents the amount of interest that would be paid if each interest-bearing issue outstanding at the end of each month or year should remain outstanding for a year at the applicable annual rate of interest. The aggregate charge for all interest-bearing issues constitutes the total computed annual interest charge. The average annual rate is compute by dividing the computed annual interest charge for the total or for any group of issues by the corresponding principal\n1574:3/ Included in debt outstanding at face amount, but discount value is used in computing annual interest charge and annual interest rate.\n1583:Table FD-3. - Interest-Bearing Public Debt\n1587:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Total interest-bearing public debt > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Marketable > Total | Marketable > Treasury bills | Marketable > Treasury notes | Marketable > Treasury bonds | Nonmarketable > Total | Nonmarketable > U.S. savings bonds |\n1717:Table FD-5. - Interest-Bearing Securities Issued by Government Agencies\n1747:Memorandum - Interest-bearing securities of non-Government agencies 2/\n1777:as agency debt. These transactions were previously reported as off-budget negative outlays. 1/ Includes unredeemed matured securities outstanding on which interest has ceased. 2/ For details of securities outstanding, see Table TSO-5.\n1859:Table FD-7. - Maturity Distribution and Average Length of Marketable Interest-Bearing Public Debt Held by Private Investors\n1891:of the interest-bearing marketable public debt is computed on that part of the outstanding public debt privately held.\n1897:| End of fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Statutory debt limit > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Debt outstanding subject to limitation > Total | Debt outstanding subject to limitation > Public debt 1/ | Debt outstanding subject to limitation > Other debt 1/ 2/ | Interest-bearing debt subject to limitation > Public debt | Interest-bearing debt subject to limitation > Other debt | Non-interest-bearing debt subject to limitation > Public debt 1/ | Non-interest-bearing debt subject to limitation > Other debt 1/ |\n1937:The Second Liberty Bond Act (31 U.S.C. 757b), as amended, provides that the face amount of obligations issued under authority of that act, and the face amount of obligations guaranteed as to principal and interest by the United States, shall not exceed in the aggregate $400 billion outstanding at any time. Public Law 97-49 provides that beginning on October 1, 1981, and ending on September 30, 1982, the statutory debt limit shall be temporarily increased by $679.8 billion.\n1939:The act of June 30, 1967 (Public Law 90-39) provides that the face amount of beneficial interests and participations issued under section 302(c) of the Federal National Mortgage Association Charter Act (12 U.S.C. 1717(c)) during the fiscal year 1968 and outstanding at any time shall be added to the amount otherwise taken into account in determining whether the requirements of the above provisions are met.\n1967:Interest-bearing debt:\n1995:| Total interest-bearing debt | nan | 1042198000 |\n1996:| Noninterest-bearing debt: | nan | nan |\n1999:| Total non-interest-bearing debt | nan | 6009000 |\n2095:Table PDO-1. - Maturity Schedule of Interest-Bearing Marketable Public Debt Securities Other than Regular Weekly and 52-Week Treasury Bills Outstanding, February 26, 1982\n2804:| Description of securities | Date of financing | Amount issued (in millions) | Average price per hundred | Effective interest rate (percent) | Eligible securities exchanged | Unnamed: 6 |\n2870:| Description of securities | Date of financing | Amount issued (in millions) | Average price per hundred | Effective interest rate (percent) | Eligible securities exchanged |\n2925:| Description of securities | Description of securities.1 | Date of financing | Amount issued (in millions) | Average price per hundred | Effective interest rate (percent) | Eligible securities exchanged |\n3014:| Description of securities | Date of financing | Amount issued (in millions) | Average price per hundred | Effective interest rate (percent) | Eligible securities exchanged |\n3285:| Securities > Unnamed: 0_level_1 | Issue date > Unnamed: 1_level_1 | Maturity date > Unnamed: 2_level_1 | Interest rate (percent) > Unnamed: 3_level_1 | Amount > Issued | Amount > Retired | Amount > Total outstanding end of month |\n3381:| Securities > Unnamed: